// Formative practice quiz for stash. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/git-foundations/stash

::stash o1 - staged and unstaged tracked changes::You have a staged change to api.js, an unstaged change to ui.js (both tracked files) and a new untracked file draft.md. You run plain git stash. What goes into the stash?{
=The api.js and ui.js changes; draft.md stays in the working copy#Correct. git stash saves changes to tracked files, staged or not. Untracked files stay behind unless you use git stash -u.
~Only the staged api.js change#Not quite. git stash takes tracked changes whether or not they are staged, so ui.js goes too.
~Only the unstaged ui.js change#Not quite. Staged changes to tracked files are stashed as well, so api.js goes too.
~All three files#Not quite. By default untracked files such as draft.md are left in the working copy.
}

::stash o1 - include untracked files::You need a clean working copy to switch branches, and the new untracked file draft.md must be set aside along with your tracked changes. Which command does that?{
=git stash -u#Correct. -u also stashes untracked files, so draft.md is set aside with the tracked changes.
~git stash, because it saves every change in the folder#Not quite. Plain git stash leaves untracked files in the working copy.
~git stash list#Not quite. git stash list only shows the entries already saved; it stashes nothing.
~git stash pop#Not quite. git stash pop brings an entry back; it does not set anything aside.
}

::stash o2 - resume on the right branch::On feature you ran git stash, switched to main, fixed a bug and committed. Now you want your unfinished feature work back where it belongs. What do you run?{
=git switch feature, then git stash pop#Correct. git stash pop reapplies the newest entry to the branch you are on now, so switch back first.
~git stash pop, then git switch feature#Not quite. Popping on main reapplies the work to main, not to feature.
~git switch feature only; the stash comes back by itself#Not quite. Git never reapplies a stash automatically. You must run git stash pop.
~git stash list, which restores the newest entry#Not quite. git stash list only shows the entries; it changes no files.
}

::stash o2 - which entry pop takes::You stashed a login form first, and later stashed a tax fix. git stash list shows stash@\{0\} and stash@\{1\}. You run git stash pop. What happens?{
=The tax fix is reapplied and removed from the stash; the login form entry stays#Correct. stash@\{0\} is the newest entry, the tax fix. Pop reapplies it and, because it applies cleanly, removes it from the stack.
~The login form is reapplied, because the first entry saved comes out first#Not quite. The stash is a stack, so the newest entry comes out first.
~Both entries are reapplied and the stash is emptied#Not quite. git stash pop takes only one entry, the newest.
~The tax fix is reapplied but also kept in the stash#Not quite. When the apply succeeds, pop removes the entry it reapplied, so the tax fix is no longer listed. Only a conflicting apply keeps the entry.
}
